      "06:29 — **What exactly to do with those items in wheat dosa?**  \nPartner, clear step-by-step. No confusion.\n\n---\n\n## 🌾 Where each ingredient goes (and why)\n\n### 🟤 1️⃣ Jeera – ½ tsp  \n👉 **Add directly into batter. Raw.**  \n- Do NOT temper separately.  \n- It releases aroma while dosa cooks.  \n- Helps digestion (wheat can feel heavy otherwise).\n\n---\n\n### 🌶️ 2️⃣ Green chilli – finely chopped  \n👉 Add into batter.  \n- If you want mild → remove seeds.  \n- If you want visible flavour pockets → don’t grind.\n\n---\n\n### 🌿 3️⃣ Curry leaves  \n👉 Finely chop and add into batter.  \n- If whole leaves → they may burn.  \n- Chopped gives aroma without bitterness.\n\n---\n\n### 🧅 4️⃣ Onion  \n👉 Add into batter **after mixing water**.  \n- Don’t grind.  \n- Spread slightly when pouring dosa so onion cooks evenly.  \n- If batter too thick, onion won’t cook properly.\n\n---\n\n### 🧈 5️⃣ Ghee  \n👉 ONLY while cooking on tawa.  \n- ½ tsp around edges.  \n- Little on top after pouring.  \n- Don’t mix ghee into batter.\n\n---\n\n## 🔥 Exact Order (Important)\n\n1. Wheat flour  \n2. Salt  \n3. Jeera  \n4. Green chilli  \n5. Curry leaves  \n6. Add water → make thin batter  \n7. Add onion last  \n8. Rest 5 mins  \n9. Cook with ghee\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake You Usually Do 😄\n- Batter too thick → raw wheat smell  \n- Onion too big → stays uncooked  \n- Low flame → becomes rubbery  \n\nStart medium-high → then reduce slightly.\n\n---\n\nIf you tell me:\nThin crispy version?  \nor  \nSoft thick version?  \n\nI’ll adjust water ratio exactly."
     ]
