      "06:57 — **Ginger in Kodumai (Wheat) Dosai?**  \nYes, partner — very good addition. Especially for digestion.\n\n---\n\n## 🌿 How to use Ginger\n\nimage_group{\"layout\":\"carousel\",\"aspect_ratio\":\"1:1\",\"query\":[\"finely chopped ginger\",\"grated fresh ginger close up\",\"wheat dosa batter with spices\",\"godhuma dosa with visible ginger pieces\"],\"num_per_query\":1}\n\n### ✅ Quantity\n- ½ tsp **finely chopped** OR grated\n\nDo NOT add too much — wheat + too much ginger = slightly bitter aftertaste.\n\n---\n\n### 🔥 Where to add?\nAdd directly into batter along with:\n- Jeera  \n- Green chilli  \n- Curry leaves  \n\nNo need to fry separately.\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Why ginger is good here\n- Cuts wheat heaviness  \n- Improves digestion  \n- Reduces gas feeling  \n- Adds mild warmth (good for UK cold mornings ❄️)\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Important\n- Chop very small (big pieces stay raw)\n- If making thin rava-style dosa → grate instead of chop\n\n---\n\n### 🔥 Your Current Best Combo (Balanced Version)\n\n- Wheat flour  \n- Salt  \n- Jeera  \n- Ginger  \n- Green chilli  \n- Onion  \n- Curry leaf  \n- Water (thin)  \n- Ghee for cooking  \n\nThis becomes a **hotel-style instant godhumai dosa**.\n\n---\n\nIf you want next level version → I can show how to make it slightly crispy without rice flour."
     ]
